WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.525
Many organizations keep their people's accounts in one identity provider.

00:00:05.525 --> 00:00:10.393
The OpenID Connect Client Integration feature lets those people sign in to your

00:00:10.393 --> 00:00:12.971
Orchard Core site with such an account.

00:00:12.971 --> 00:00:16.980
In this video, we connect a provider and follow a first sign-in.

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:26.067
An identity provider is a server that knows your users and signs them in.

00:00:26.067 --> 00:00:31.065
With the OpenID Connect Client Integration feature, your site sends people to that

00:00:31.065 --> 00:00:33.167
provider and trusts its answer.

00:00:33.167 --> 00:00:37.370
Orchard Core can also be the provider; a separate video covers that.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:42.302
The provider gives you an authority, which is its address, a client id

00:00:42.302 --> 00:00:45.218
that identifies your site, and a client secret.

00:00:45.218 --> 00:00:49.620
It also needs to know the address on your site where it sends people back.

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:54.642
On the login page, a button for the provider sits beside the usual form.

00:00:54.642 --> 00:00:58.640
The person signs in at the provider, which sends them back, and the

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:01.050
site signs them in to a local account.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:05.971
When the person comes back, a linked account signs in directly.

00:01:05.971 --> 00:01:10.564
A matching email address asks for the local password, to link the two.

00:01:10.564 --> 00:01:15.460
Anything else creates an account, unless external registration is disabled.

00:01:19.752 --> 00:01:23.808
Let's open Tools, Features, and search for OpenID Connect.

00:01:23.808 --> 00:01:31.412
The OpenID Connect Client Integration feature allows people to authenticate through an external identity provider.

00:01:31.867 --> 00:01:33.076
We click Enable.

00:01:33.076 --> 00:01:39.067
The feature depends on OpenID Connect Core Services and External Authentication, which turn on with it.

00:01:43.367 --> 00:01:47.886
The settings are under Settings, OpenID Connect, Authentication Client.

00:01:47.886 --> 00:01:51.583
Display Name is the text of the button on the login page.

00:01:51.583 --> 00:01:53.637
Let's enter Example Identity.

00:01:59.072 --> 00:02:04.194
Authority is the address of the provider, and ClientId is the identifier it

00:02:04.194 --> 00:02:05.964
assigned to your site.

00:02:05.964 --> 00:02:09.822
Display Name, Authority, and ClientId are all required.

00:02:10.284 --> 00:02:14.418
CallbackPath is the path on your site where the provider sends people back

00:02:14.418 --> 00:02:15.709
after they sign in.

00:02:15.709 --> 00:02:20.464
We enter the path the field suggests, which the provider has registered for this site.

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:26.297
Scopes lists what the site asks the provider to share, beyond openid and profile.

00:02:26.297 --> 00:02:30.600
We add email, so the provider sends each person's email address.

00:02:31.063 --> 00:02:34.968
SignedOut CallbackPath, SignedOut Redirect Uri, and Store tokens?

00:02:34.968 --> 00:02:36.013
are optional.

00:02:36.013 --> 00:02:40.413
The first two control where people go after signing out, and Store tokens?

00:02:40.413 --> 00:02:42.283
keeps the provider's tokens.

00:02:42.283 --> 00:02:44.043
We leave them as they are.

00:02:50.593 --> 00:02:52.146
Next comes the flow.

00:02:52.146 --> 00:02:58.893
The checkboxes under Code Authentication Flow, Hybrid Authentication Flow, and Implicit Authentication Flow

00:02:58.893 --> 00:03:02.535
are a single choice, so checking one clears the others.

00:03:02.535 --> 00:03:04.923
We check Use 'code' response type.

00:03:05.455 --> 00:03:08.839
The flows that use a code show the Client Secret field.

00:03:08.839 --> 00:03:11.723
We enter the secret that the provider gave us.

00:03:11.723 --> 00:03:13.665
Response Mode stays as it is.

00:03:14.129 --> 00:03:15.199
We click Save.

00:03:15.199 --> 00:03:19.960
The warning at the top says that the website might restart when settings are saved.

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:24.722
A message confirms that the site settings were updated successfully, and the Client

00:03:24.722 --> 00:03:27.879
Secret field shows that its value is securely stored.

00:03:32.111 --> 00:03:36.848
Settings, Security, Registration has External Authentication options.

00:03:36.848 --> 00:03:41.901
They apply when someone signs in with a provider and has no local account.

00:03:42.386 --> 00:03:47.522
Disable new external registration lets only people with a local account sign in.

00:03:47.522 --> 00:03:51.942
On this site it is selected, so first-time users could not register.

00:03:51.942 --> 00:03:56.122
Do not ask username, Do not ask email address, and Do not create

00:03:56.122 --> 00:03:59.406
local password let the site skip those questions.

00:04:00.023 --> 00:04:05.243
Let's clear Disable new external registration, check Do not ask email address and

00:04:05.243 --> 00:04:08.303
Do not create local password, and click Save.

00:04:08.303 --> 00:04:13.823
Because the provider sends the email address, first-time users only choose a username.

00:04:15.423 --> 00:04:19.029
Settings, Security, User Login has two more options.

00:04:19.029 --> 00:04:25.307
Use external provider for login sends people straight to the provider when only one is defined.

00:04:25.307 --> 00:04:32.083
Disable local password login removes the password form, so keep an administrator with a linked account.

00:04:36.342 --> 00:04:40.201
A visitor who is signed out sees the usual login form, and a

00:04:40.201 --> 00:04:43.182
section called Use another service to log in.

00:04:43.182 --> 00:04:47.332
Its button carries the Display Name we entered, Example Identity.

00:04:47.859 --> 00:04:49.626
We click Example Identity.

00:04:49.626 --> 00:04:53.879
The browser leaves the site and opens the sign-in page of the provider.

00:04:53.879 --> 00:04:57.799
In this recording, a second Orchard Core site plays the provider.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:00.777
We sign in as Jamie Chen at the provider.

00:05:00.777 --> 00:05:05.328
The provider checks the credentials, and sends the browser back to the callback

00:05:05.328 --> 00:05:06.560
path of our site.

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:11.868
The site finds no local account for this person, so it shows a

00:05:11.868 --> 00:05:13.926
page called Create a new account.

00:05:13.926 --> 00:05:17.515
It asks only for a username, and fills in a suggestion that we

00:05:17.515 --> 00:05:18.940
replace with our own.

00:05:19.435 --> 00:05:20.736
We click Register.

00:05:20.736 --> 00:05:24.963
The site creates the account, links it to the provider, signs the person

00:05:24.963 --> 00:05:26.805
in, and opens the home page.

00:05:31.079 --> 00:05:35.749
Back in the admin, Access Control, Users lists the account, with the username

00:05:35.749 --> 00:05:38.619
and the email address that the provider sent.

00:05:39.082 --> 00:05:43.681
The next time this person chooses Example Identity, the site finds the linked

00:05:43.681 --> 00:05:47.172
account and signs them in, without the registration page.

00:05:49.172 --> 00:05:54.196
The OpenID Connect Client Integration feature hands the sign-in to an identity provider,

00:05:54.196 --> 00:05:56.762
and keeps a local account for each person.

00:05:56.762 --> 00:05:58.152
Thanks for watching.

